\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Example}
\title{Signed and zero weights on a one-vertex circle}
\date{2026-11-17}

\p{Use the sign representation of #{C_2=\{1,-1\}} over
#{\mathbb Q} on the cellular circle with one vertex. The
degree-zero and degree-one coefficients give
##{\extD_U=\begin{pmatrix}0&a_U\\a_U&0\end{pmatrix},
 \qquad a_U=U^{-1}-1.}
Thus the determinants of #{m\bbI+\extD_U} at #{U=1,-1}
are respectively #{m^2} and #{m^2-4}. With weight one and
#{m=1},
##{Z=1-3=-2,\qquad
 \nu(1)=-\tfrac12,\quad\nu(-1)=\tfrac32.}
The [normalized coefficient](fdip-00TU) exists over
#{\mathbb Q}, but these connection weights are not a
probability law. At #{m=2}, the two partition weights are
#{4} and #{0}; the second kernel is singular. The
unnormalized insertion formulas in \ref{fdip-00TQ} still apply.}
